<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gonur Depe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2400–1600 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/25th-century-bce">25th century BCE</a> · Also known as Goňur depe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gonur Depe is the site of a large early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> settlement about 60 km north of Mary, ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>, in Turkmenistan, dated from 2400 to 1600 BCE and regarded as the major settlement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margiana">Margiana</a> Archaeological Complex. Viktor Sarianidi, who discovered it, excavated it from 1974 to 2013 and uncovered a palace, a fortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> enclosure and temples with fire altars, which he associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>. Its Royal Necropolis held gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and bronze goods and a foal buried on a four-wheeled wagon, radiocarbon-dated to 2250 BCE, and its Large Necropolis more than three thousand graves. The site was most likely abandoned after the Murghab River shifted to the west.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gonur-depe.jpg" alt="Gonur Depe" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Stanley · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gonur_ruins.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Gonur Depe</b>, in the Murghab delta of southern Turkmenistan, was the largest settlement, about 55 hectares, and Sarianidi regarded it as the “capital” of Margiana.</blockquote><a href="/culture/oxus-civilisation">Oxus civilisation · Society</a></li></ul></section>
